<div class="page">
  <header style="text-align: center;">
    <p style="font-size: 10pt;">2</p>
    <h1 style="font-size: 14pt; margin-top: 0;">ULRIKE TILLMANN</h1>
  </header>
  <div class="section-title">1. A historical introduction</div>
  <p>This is in essence an expanded version of my talk at a conference in honor of <span data-field="historical_person_1">{{ historical_person_1 }}</span> on the occasion of his 70th birthday in 2007. Coincidentally it was also the 150th anniversary of Riemann's influential paper <span data-field="historical_paper_title">{{ historical_paper_title }}</span> [R], the starting point for the study of moduli spaces.</p>
  <p>In his paper <span data-field="historical_paper_ref">{{ historical_paper_ref }}</span> considers how the complex structure of the surfaces associated to a multi-valued complex function changes when one continuously varies the parameters of the function. He concludes that when the genus of the surface is $g \ge 2$ the isomorphism class depends on $3g-3$ complex variables, which he calls "moduli" of the classes. Thus Riemann understands the complex dimension of his space to be $3g-3$. He also introduces its name, moduli space, into the mathematical literature.</p>
  <p>The moduli space <span data-field="moduli_space_name">{{ moduli_space_name }}</span> of Riemann surfaces (or complex curves) of genus $g$ has several constructions. One of these comes from complex analysis via Teichmüller spaces. We will discuss this in more detail below as it relates easily to the topological point of view. But it was Mumford who introduced <span data-field="mumford_concept">{{ mumford_concept }}</span> into algebraic geometry. A precise definition and construction of the coarse moduli space for smooth complex curves was given in his far-reaching 1965 book <span data-field="mumford_book_title">{{ mumford_book_title }}</span> [MI]. A few years later he constructed a natural compactification <span data-field="compactification_name">{{ compactification_name }}</span> in his paper with Deligne [DM]. In this construction, the added points in the compactification again correspond to natural geometric objects, so called stable curves.</p>
  <p>The moduli space and its compactification were much studied by algebraic geometers from this time on, but for progress on the (co)homology of <span data-field="moduli_space_name">{{ moduli_space_name }}</span> we have to wait until the early 1980s when we suddenly see several developments in topology and geometry at the same time.</p>
  <p>On the algebraic geometric side, Mumford initiates the systematic study of the Chow ring of <span data-field="moduli_space_name">{{ moduli_space_name }}</span> and <span data-field="moduli_space_name">{{ moduli_space_name }}</span> in his 1983 paper <span data-field="mumford_paper_title">{{ mumford_paper_title }}</span> [M2]. The idea here is, we quote, "to take as a model for this the enumerative geometry of the Grassmannians", and in analogy to the Chern classes Mumford defines and studies certain tautological classes <span data-field="tautological_classes">{{ tautological_classes }}</span>.</p>
  <p>The rational cohomology of <span data-field="moduli_space_name">{{ moduli_space_name }}</span> is the same as that of the mapping class group $\Gamma_g$, the group of isotopy classes of diffeomorphisms of a surface of genus $g$, as we will explain below. This group in turn has been studied extensively in algebra and topology. At around the same time as Mumford studied the Chow ring of <span data-field="moduli_space_name">{{ moduli_space_name }}</span>, Hatcher and Thurston [HT] found a presentation for $\Gamma_g$. This work then led to ground-breaking work by Harer. He computed the second cohomology (thus confirming a conjecture by Mumford on the Picard group of the moduli spaces) [H1], and, what is more important for this paper, proved in [H2] that their cohomology is independent of the genus $g$ in degrees small relative to $g$. Miller [Mi] in turn, and independently Morita [Mo], used this stability result to show that in the stable range the rational cohomology contains a polynomial algebra on Mumford's <span data-field="tautological_classes">{{ tautological_classes }}</span> classes.</p>
  <p>“... it seems reasonable to guess, in view of the results of Harer and Miller, that in low dimensions $H^*(\text{M}_g) \otimes \mathbb{Q}$ is a polynomial algebra in the <span data-field="tautological_classes">{{ tautological_classes }}</span>,”</p>
</div>

<div class="page">
  <header style="text-align: center;">
    <p style="font-size: 10pt;">3</p>
    <h1 style="font-size: 18pt; margin-top: 0;">MUMFORD'S CONJECTURE - A TOPOLOGICAL OUTLOOK</h1>
  </header>
  <p>This has since been known as the Mumford conjecture and is now a theorem due to Madsen and Weiss [MW].</p>
  <p>We will explain some of the ideas that led to the proof of Mumford's conjecture. The first proof is a tour de force. Since then the arguments have been simplified significantly in [GMTW]. Our discussion will be based on that.</p>
  <div class="section-title">Acknowledgements:</div>
  <p>I am grateful to <span data-field="acknowledgement_person_1">{{ acknowledgement_person_1 }}</span> for encouraging me to write this paper and to the editors of this handbook for their patience. I would also like to thank the referee for many valuable suggestions.</p>
  <div class="section-title">2. Topological models for <span data-field="moduli_space_name">{{ moduli_space_name }}</span> and <span data-field="moduli_space_name">{{ moduli_space_name }}</span></div>
  <p>The purpose of this section is to construct a topological moduli space <span data-field="topological_moduli_space">{{ topological_moduli_space }}</span> of surfaces and compare it with Riemann's moduli space. It will have the property that any smooth map $f: X \to \text{M}_g^{top}$ defines a smooth bundle of genus $g$ surfaces over a manifold $X$. Indeed, $\text{M}_g^{top}$ is the space of un-parameterized genus $g$ surfaces smoothly embedded in $\mathbb{R}^n$.</p>
  <div class="section-title">2.1. Construction.</div>
  <p>The construction of the topological moduli space can be made quite generally for any closed orientable manifold $W$. We let $\text{Emb}(W;\mathbb{R}^n)$ be the space of smooth embeddings of $W$ in $n$-dimensional real Euclidean space with the $C^\infty$-topology, and let</p>
  <div class="equation">
    $\text{Emb}(W;\mathbb{R}^n) := \lim_{k \to \infty} \text{Emb}(W;\mathbb{R}^k)$
  </div>
  <p>be the union of these. It is the space of all smooth parameterized sub-manifolds in $\mathbb{R}^n$ diffeomorphic to $W$. By the Whitney embedding theorem [W], $\text{Emb}(W;\mathbb{R}^n)$ is contractible. The group of orientation preserving diffeomorphisms $\text{Diff}(W)$ of $W$ acts freely on it by precomposition: for a diffeomorphism $\phi$ and an embedding $h$ we define $h \circ \phi = h \circ \phi$. The topological moduli space is the associated orbit space,</p>
  <div class="equation">
    $\text{M}_W^{top} := \text{Emb}(W;\mathbb{R}^n) / \text{Diff}(W)$
  </div>
  <p>the space of all smooth un-parameterized sub-manifolds of $\mathbb{R}^n$ diffeomorphic to $W$. Its associated universal $W$-bundle is given by the Borel construction</p>
  <div class="equation">
    $\text{Emb}(W;\mathbb{R}^n) \times_{\text{Diff}(W)} W \to \text{M}_W^{top}(W)$
  </div>
  <p>which restricted to $\text{Emb}(W;\mathbb{R}^n)$ is a smooth fiber bundle in the infinite dimensional smooth category (see [KM, Section 44]). In particular, our topological moduli space</p>
</div>

<div class="page">
  <header style="text-align: center;">
    <p style="font-size: 10pt;">4</p>
    <h1 style="font-size: 14pt; margin-top: 0;">ULRIKE TILLMANN</h1>
  </header>
  <p>is a classifying space for the group of diffeomorphisms,</p>
  <div class="equation">
    $\text{M}_W^{top}(W) \simeq \text{BDiff}(W)$
  </div>
  <p>We need a variant of the above construction when $W$ has non-empty boundary. More precisely, we will be a cobordism between an incoming manifold $\text{M}_0$ and an outgoing manifold $\text{M}_1$. In other words, the boundary of $W$ is subdivided into two parts such that $\partial W = \text{M}_0 \cup \text{M}_1$, where $\text{M}_0$ denotes the manifold $\text{M}_0$ with the opposite orientation. Consider the space of smooth embeddings $\text{Emb}(\text{M}_0 \cup \text{M}_1; \mathbb{R}^n)$ and similarly $\text{Emb}(\text{M}_0 \cup \text{M}_1; \mathbb{R}^n)$. The group of diffeomorphisms $\text{Diff}(\text{M}_0 \cup \text{M}_1)$ of $W$ that map $\text{M}_0$ to $\text{M}_0$ and $\text{M}_1$ to $\text{M}_1$ preserves a collar acts freely on this space of embeddings. Define</p>
  <div class="equation">
    $\text{M}_{W}^{cob} := \text{Emb}(W;\mathbb{R}^n) / \text{Diff}(W)$
  </div>
  <p>Again, the embedding space is contractible and $\text{M}_{W}^{cob} \simeq \text{BDiff}(W)$. The homotopy type of $\text{BDiff}(W)$ is the classifying space of the group of diffeomorphisms that have a fixed image of $\text{M}_0$ and $\text{M}_1$, the resulting orbit space has the homotopy type of $\text{BDiff}(W, \partial)$, the classifying space of those diffeomorphisms that fix a collar of the boundary pointwise, see [MT, Section 2]. We will in particular be interested in the case when $W$ is an oriented surface $\Sigma_{g,n}$ of genus $g$ with $n$ boundary components.</p>
  <div class="figure-caption">
    Figure 1: Embedded cobordism $W$ with $\partial W = \text{M}_0 \cup \text{M}_1$.
  </div>
  <div class="section-title">2.2. The relation to Riemann's moduli space <span data-field="moduli_space_name">{{ moduli_space_name }}</span>.</div>
  <p>There are several constructions of the moduli space of Riemann surfaces $\text{M}_g$. We have already mentioned the construction by Mumford via geometric invariant theory [MI]. A somewhat different approach is via Teichmüller theory. Let $\Sigma_{g,n}$ be a surface with $n$ boundary components. The Teichmüller space $\mathcal{T}_{g,n}$ is the space of conformal structures on the oriented surface $\Sigma_{g,n}$ which are compatible with the orientation. This is the space of sections of the $\text{GL}(2, \mathbb{R})/\text{GL}(2, \mathbb{Z})$ bundle associated to the tangent bundle of $\Sigma_{g,n}$. On surfaces, every almost complex structure integrates to a complex structure.</p>
  <p>Let $\text{Diff}_+(\Sigma_{g,n})$ denote the connected component of the identity in $\text{Diff}(\Sigma_{g,n})$. This is a normal subgroup and its quotient is the mapping class group $\Gamma_{g,n} := \pi_0(\text{Diff}_+(\Sigma_{g,n}))$. If we define Teichmüller space as</p>
  <div class="equation">
    $\mathcal{T}_{g,n} := \text{Diff}_+(\Sigma_{g,n}) / \text{Diff}_0(\Sigma_{g,n})$
  </div>
  <p>then</p>
  <div class="equation">
    $\text{M}_g = \mathcal{T}_{g,n} / \Gamma_{g,n}$
  </div>
  <p>It is well-known that $\mathcal{T}_{g,n}$ is homeomorphic to $\mathbb{R}^{6g-6}$ and hence contractible. Furthermore, Earle and Eells [EE] show that the natural projection from $\mathcal{T}_{g,n}$ to $\Gamma_{g,n}$ has local sections so that</p>
  <div class="equation">
    $\text{Diff}_+(\Sigma_{g,n}) \to \mathcal{T}_{g,n} \to \text{M}_g$
  </div>
  <p>is a fiber bundle. It follows that the fiber $\text{Diff}_+(\Sigma_{g,n})$ is contractible as both $\text{S}(\mathcal{T}_{g,n})$ and $\mathcal{T}_{g,n}$ are. On the other hand, the group of holomorphic self-maps of a surface is finite (of order less than $84(g-1)$ by Hurwitz' formula). Thus all the stabilizer groups of the action of $\Gamma_{g,n}$ on $\mathcal{T}_{g,n}$ are finite. Furthermore, the action is proper. One can use this to prove that $\text{M}_g$ has the same rational cohomology as the classifying space $\text{B}\Gamma_{g,n}$. Hence,</p>
  <div class="equation">
    $\text{M}_g^{top} \simeq \text{BDiff}(\Sigma_{g,n}) \simeq \text{B}\Gamma_{g,n} \simeq \text{M}_g$
  </div>
  <p>Here $\simeq$ indicates that the two spaces have isomorphic rational cohomology.</p>
  <p>The constructions above can be extended to define a moduli space $\text{M}_g^{top,n}$ of surfaces with $n$ punctures and $n$ marked points with a unit tangent vector. For this we need to consider the group of diffeomorphisms that fix the marked points as well as the unit vectors. The associated mapping class group is denoted by $\Gamma_{g,n,1}$. Earle and Eells' result also applies to this case whenever $2g-2+n < 0$, see [EE]. Holomorphic self-maps of degree one that fix a point and a tangent vector at that point have to be the identity (by the identity theorem). Thus for $n>0$, the stabilizer subgroups for the action of this mapping class group on the corresponding Teichmüller space are all trivial. Similarly, the maximal number of fixed points of a holomorphic map of a surface of genus $g$ is $2g+2$, and thus again for $k>2g+2$ the stabilizer subgroups are trivial. We thus have the following homotopy equivalences</p>
  <div class="equation">
    $\text{M}_{g,n}^{top} \simeq \text{BDiff}(\Sigma_{g,n,1}) \simeq \text{B}\Gamma_{g,n,1} \simeq \text{M}_{g,n}$
  </div>
  <p>when $n>0$ or $k>2g+2$; otherwise the equivalence $\simeq$ on the right has to be replaced again by a rational equivalence $\simeq_{\mathbb{Q}}$.</p>
</div>

<div class="page">
  <header style="text-align: center;">
    <p style="font-size: 10pt;">5</p>
    <h1 style="font-size: 14pt; margin-top: 0;">ULRIKE TILLMANN</h1>
  </header>
  <div class="section-title">2.3. Cobordism categories.</div>
  <p>As we will see in section 4.4, a key new idea in studying the topology of moduli spaces was to study them all together as part of a category. The motivation for this came from conformal and topological field theory.</p>
  <p>Segal's category: Segal's axiomatic approach [S4] to conformal field theory is based on a symmetric monoidal category $\mathcal{S}$ of Riemann surfaces. It has one object for each natural number $n$ representing $n$ disjoint copies of the unit circle. Its space of morphisms from $n$ to $m$ is the union of the moduli spaces of complex surfaces (possibly not connected) of genus $g$ with $n$ source and $m$ target boundary components, and in particular contains all moduli spaces $\text{M}_{g,n}$ for $g \ge 0$. The composition is given by gluing, and the monoidal structure is given by disjoint union, which is clearly symmetric. In Segal's setting a conformal field theory is a symmetric monoidal functor from $\mathcal{S}$ to an appropriate category of topological vector spaces in which the monoidal structure is defined by the tensor product.</p>
  <div class="section-title">Cobordism categories.</div>
  <p>In a similar way, the topological moduli spaces also form a category, which we denote by $\text{Cob}_g$. In $\text{Cob}_g$, an embedded $d$-dimensional cobordism $W$ as in figure 1 is a morphism between its boundary components $\text{M}_0$ and $\text{M}_1$. More precisely, the space of objects in $\text{Cob}_g$ is the union over all $g \ge 0$ of the space of embedded $(d-1)$-dimensional closed, oriented manifolds in $\mathbb{R}^n$ with $\text{M}_0$ and $\text{M}_1$ being the spaces of morphisms is the union for all intervals $[a_0, a_1]$ of the spaces of cobordisms between $\text{M}_0$ and $\text{M}_1$ (see [MT, Section 2]). Two morphisms $W$ and $W'$ are equivalent if there is a diffeomorphism $\phi: W \to W'$ that maps $\text{M}_0$ to $\text{M}_0$ and $\text{M}_1$ to $\text{M}_1$ such that the same source boundary $\text{M}_0$ of $W'$. In particular, we must have $\text{M}_0 = \text{M}_0'$ and $\text{M}_1 = \text{M}_1'$. The composition is then given by gluing the two cobordism along their common boundary:</p>
  <div class="equation">
    $W \circ W' := \text{Cob}(W, W') \in \text{Cob}_g$
  </div>
  <p>Given two objects $\text{M}_0$ and $\text{M}_1$ (with $\text{M}_0$ with $a_0 < a_1$ the space of morphisms between them is</p>
  <div class="equation">
    $\text{Cob}(\text{M}_0, \text{M}_1) := \text{BDiff}(W, \partial)$
  </div>
  <p>where the disjoint union is taken over all diffeomorphism classes of cobordisms $W$ with boundary $\text{M}_0 \cup \text{M}_1$. In particular, when $d=2$ and $\text{M}_0$ and $\text{M}_1$ consist of respectively $n$ and $m$ circles, this will contain spaces homotopic to $\text{B}\Gamma_{g,n+m}$ for all $g \ge 0$.</p>
  <p>The category $\text{Cob}_g$ is a model for the source category of $d$-dimensional topological field theories. Such theories are in an appropriate sense symmetric monoidal functor from $\text{Cob}_g$ to a category of vector spaces or some generalization thereof. We refer to [L] where these theories are studied in detail. Theorem 7.1 below formed the inspiration for the main theorem in [L] that classifies so called extended topological field theories.</p>
</div>

<div class="page">
  <header style="text-align: center;">
    <p style="font-size: 10pt;">7</p>
    <h1 style="font-size: 14pt; margin-top: 0;">ULRIKE TILLMANN</h1>
  </header>
  <div class="section-title">3. Classifying spaces and group completion - a tutorial</div>
  <div class="section-title">3.1. The nerve of a category.</div>
  <p>Like moduli spaces, classifying spaces of groups are representing spaces: The set of homotopy classes of maps from a space $X$ to the classifying space $\text{BG}$ of a group $G$ is one to one correspondence with the set of isomorphism classes of principal $G$-bundles on $X$. In particular, $\text{BG}$ has a universal $G$-bundle (corresponding to the identity map).</p>
  <p>We will present a functorial construction that can be generalized to categories. (A group $G$ is identified with the category containing one object $\ast$ and morphism set $G$).</p>
  <p>The nerve $\text{N}(\mathcal{C})$ of a category $\mathcal{C}$ is a simplicial space with 0-simplices the space of objects and $n$-simplices the space of $n$-composable morphisms. Boundary maps are given by composition of morphisms:</p>
  <div class="equation">
    $\partial_i (\text{f}_0, \dots, \text{f}_n) = (\text{f}_0, \dots, \text{f}_i \circ \text{f}_{i+1}, \dots, \text{f}_n)$
  </div>
  <p>when $i \ne 0, n$, and $\partial_0$ and $\partial_n$ drop the first respectively last coordinate. The $i$-th degeneracy map is defined by inserting an identity morphism at the $i$-th place. Recall, every simplicial space $X$ has a realization</p>
  <div class="equation">
    $|X| := \left| \bigcup_{n \ge 0} \Delta^n \times X_n \right| / \sim$
  </div>
  <p>where the identifications are generated by the boundary and degeneracy maps. $|X|$ is given the compactly generated topology induced by the topology of the standard $n$-simplex $\Delta^n$ and the topology on $X_n$. The classifying space of a category is the realization of its nerve.</p>
  <div class="section-title">BC := $|\text{N}(\mathcal{C})|$.</div>
  <p>It is not difficult to see that this construction is functorial. It takes functors between categories to continuous maps between their classifying spaces. Furthermore, natural transformations between two functors induce homotopies between the induced maps. These construction go back to Grothendieck and in our topological setting to Segal [S1].</p>
  <div class="section-title">Proposition 3.1.</div>
  <p>If $\mathcal{C}$ has a terminal object, then its classifying space $\text{BC}$ is contractible.</p>
  <div class="section-title">3.2. A functorial map and group completion.</div>
  <p>Unlike in the case of a group $G$, it is not so easy to see what $\text{BC}$ classifies for an arbitrary category, and we will not pursue this question here. Nevertheless we want to study the closely related question of how much information the classifying space retains about the category.</p>
  <p>Let $(\mathcal{C}, \mathcal{B})$ be the space of morphisms between two objects $a$ and $b$. From the definition of the classifying space in section 3.1, we see there is a natural map from $\Delta^1 \times \mathcal{C}(a,b)$ to $\text{BC}$. The adjoint map defines the important map</p>
  <div class="equation">
    $\sigma: \mathcal{C}(a,b) \to \Omega \text{BC}$
  </div>
  <p>which associates to every morphism in $\mathcal{C}(a,b)$ a path in $\text{BC}$ from the point corresponding to the source $a$ to the one corresponding to the target $b$.</p>
  <p>When $\mathcal{C}$ is the group $G$, $\sigma$ gives the well-known homotopy equivalence</p>
  <div class="equation">
    $G \simeq \Omega \text{BG}$
  </div>
  <p>For example, we have $\text{B}\mathbb{Z} \simeq \mathbb{R}/\mathbb{Z} \simeq \text{S}^1$ and hence $\Omega \text{B}\mathbb{Z} \simeq \mathbb{Z}$. The above also holds for groups up to homotopy, i.e. when $\mathcal{C}$ is a monoid $\mathcal{M}$ whose connected components $\pi_0 \mathcal{M}$ form a group. Thus in these cases the classifying space contains up to homotopy the same information as the category.</p>
  <p>More generally, when $\mathcal{C}$ is a monoid, $\sigma$ is the group completion map. Recall, every (discrete) monoid $\mathcal{M}$ has a group completion, its Grothendieck group $\text{G}(\mathcal{M})$. For the free monoid $\mathcal{M} = \mathbb{N}$, one adds the inverses to obtain $\text{G}(\mathbb{N}) = \mathbb{Z}$. More generally, one can construct</p>
  <div class="equation">
    $\text{G}(\mathcal{M}) \simeq \text{M} \setminus \text{M}_1 \cup \text{M}_2 \cup \text{M}_3 \cup \text{M}_4 \in \text{M}$
  </div>
</div>




