  <p>Note that the two spaces are generally not homotopy equivalent. While a loop space has always abelian fundamental group, <span data-field="object_name_7">{{ object_name_7 }}</span> has generally a very complicated and non-abelian fundamental group. But in many cases of interest the spaces <span data-field="object_name_8">{{ object_name_8 }}</span> and <span data-field="object_name_9">{{ object_name_9 }}</span> are related by the plus construction. Recall, when the fundamental group <span data-field="group_name">{{ group_name }}</span> of a space <span data-field="space_name">{{ space_name }}</span> has perfect commutator subgroup <span data-field="group_name_2">{{ group_name_2 }}</span> one may apply Quillen's plus construction which attaches to each generator of the perfect commutator subgroup a disk and furthermore attaches certain 3-cells so that the homology remains unchanged. The resulting space <span data-field="space_name_2">{{ space_name_2 }}</span> has now fundamental group <span data-field="group_name_3">{{ group_name_3 }}</span> and the same homology as <span data-field="space_name">{{ space_name }}</span>.</p>

