  <p>The part of the homology that does not change when increasing the genus or changing the number of boundary components is called stable. The homology stability theorem for mapping class groups was first proved by John Harer [H2]. Subsequently, Ivanov [I] and most recently Belden [B] and Randall-Williams [RW1] improved the stable range. The quoted range is the best known and (essentially) best possible. For a more precise statement see also Wahl's survey in this volume [Wai2].</p>
  <p>We are led to study the limit group</p>
