  <p>Proof. The product is commutative up to conjugation by an element <span data-field="element_gamma">{{ element_gamma }}</span> in <span data-field="group_name_6">{{ group_name_6 }}</span> that interchanges the two 'legs' in Figure 2 by a half twist of the 'waist'. It is a standard fact from group cohomology, see [Br], that conjugation induces the identity map in homology. Thus the product is commutative in homology and the group completion theorem 3.2 can be applied. This gives the claimed identity of homology groups.</p>
  <p>But more is true. The element <span data-field="element_beta">{{ element_beta }}</span> can be interpreted as a braid element in the mapping class group of the pair of pants surface (in this context, we allow the interchanging of boundary components). With this it is now formal to prove that the monoid <span data-field="monoid_M_3">{{ monoid_M_3 }}</span> is a braided category and its group completion is a double loop space according to the table (3.4). See also [FS].</p>
  <p>The homology of each <span data-field="object_name_29">{{ object_name_29 }}</span> is finitely generated (indeed one can construct mod-els for <span data-field="object_name_29">{{ object_name_29 }}</span> that are finite cell complexes, see for example [Bo]). Harer's homology stability theorem implies that the homology of <span data-field="object_name_29">{{ object_name_29 }}</span> is finitely generated in every degree, i.e. is of finite type. Rationally, finite type commutative and co-commutative Hopf algebras are polynomial algebras on even degree generators tensored exterior algebras on odd degree generators by the structure theorem of Milnor-Moore [MM]. To prove the theorem below, it is thus enough to show that the <span data-field="object_name_29">{{ object_name_29 }}</span> is a finite complex, which is true by the work of [Mil]. He shows that <span data-field="object_name_29">{{ object_name_29 }}</span> vanishes on decomposable elements in the homology and extends a method of Atiyah [A] to construct inductively surfaces bundles over 2d-dimensional manifolds (indeed, smooth projective algebraic varieties) with non-zero <span data-field="number_k">{{ number_k }}</span> number. Independently, in [Mo] Morita proves the same theorem by a more direct method.</p>
