<div class="page">
  <header style="display: flex; justify-content: space-between; align-items: center; margin-bottom: 0.5em;">
    <h1 style="font-size: 14pt; margin: 0;">MUMFORD'S CONJECTURE - A TOPOLOGICAL OUTLOOK</h1>
    <span style="font-size: 12pt;">{{ page_number }}</span>
  </header>

  <div style="margin-top: 1em;">
    <h3 style="font-size: 12pt; font-weight: bold;">Proposition 5.3.</h3>
    <p>
      <span data-field="proposition_text">{{ proposition_text }}</span>
      <div class="equation">
        H*(BSO(2)) = H*(CP^∞) ≅ Z[e] with deg(e) = 2.
      </div>
    </p>
    <p>
      Thus there is one polynomial generator <span data-field="generator_name">{{ generator_name }}</span> in H*(Ω^MSO(2)) ⊗ Q for each e1, e2, ... . The Mumford conjecture thus follows. (We will explain in more detail how k_i is related to e^{i+1} in section 7.1, once we have defined the map α.)
    </p>
  </div>
</div>

<div class="page">
  <div style="margin-top: 1em;">
    <h3 style="font-size: 12pt; font-weight: bold;">Corollary 5.4.</h3>
    <p>
      <span data-field="corollary_text">{{ corollary_text }}</span>
    </p>
    <p>
      Proof of Proposition 5.3: A result of Serre [S] says that for an (n-1)-connected, compact space X the Hurwitz map πk(X) → H_k(X) is a rational isomorphism in degrees k < 2n - 1. Note that the Thom space of an n-dimensional vector bundle is (n-1)-connected. We apply first Serre's result and then the Thom isomorphism theorem to get: for a fixed k and large enough n,
    </p>
    <div class="equation">
      πk(Ω^MSO(d)) ⊗ Q = Q[πk(Ω^{d+n}Th(U_n)) ⊗ Q]
    </div>
    <p>
      = H_{k+d+n}(Gr(d, n)) ⊗ Q
      = H_{k+d}(BSO(d)) ⊗ Q.
    </p>
    <p>
      By a theorem of Milnor and Moore [MM], the rational homology of any connected double loop space X is the free graded commutative algebra on its rational homotopy groups.
    </p>
    <div class="equation">
      Λ(π_*(X) ⊗ Q) ≅ H_*(X, Q).
    </div>
    <p>
      By taking duals we get the result of the proposition.
    </p>
  </div>
</div>

<div class="page">
  <div style="margin-top: 1em;">
    <h3 style="font-size: 12pt; font-weight: bold;">6. Divisibility and torsion in the stable (co)homology.</h3>
    <p>
      The generalized Mumford conjecture gives much more information than just the rational information. Indeed, it gives us exactly as much stable information as we can understand about the space Ω^MSO(2). The limits to this are essentially the same as the limits of our understanding of stable homotopy theory itself.
    </p>

    <h4 style="font-size: 11pt; margin-top: 1em;">6.1. Divisibility of the k_i classes.</h4>
    <p>
      Harer in [H1] proved that <span data-field="harer_result">{{ harer_result }}</span> To generalize this result to higher k_i classes we will work modulo torsion and write
    </p>
    <div class="equation">
      H_free(BT^∞) := H^*(BT^∞; Z)/Torsion
    </div>
    <p>
      for the integral lattice in H^*(BT^∞; Q). The following theorem was proved in [GMT].
    </p>
  </div>
</div>

<div class="page">
  <div style="margin-top: 1em;">
    <h3 style="font-size: 12pt; font-weight: bold;">18</h3>
    <p style="text-align: right; font-size: 10pt;">ULRIKE TILLMANN</p>
  </div>

  <div style="margin-top: 1em;">
    <h3 style="font-size: 12pt; font-weight: bold;">Theorem 6.1.</h3>
    <p>
      Let D_i be the maximal divisor of k_i in H_free(BT^∞). Then for all i ≥ 1
    </p>
    <div class="equation">
      D_{2i} = 2 \quad \text{and} \quad D_{2i-1} = \text{Den}(\frac{B_i}{2i}).
    </div>
    <p>
      As before, B_i denotes the i-th Bernoulli number and Den is the function that takes a rational number when expressed as a fraction in its lowest terms to its denominator. By a theorem of von Staudt it is well-known that Den(B_i) is the product of all primes p such that p-1 divides 2i, and that a prime divides Den(B_i/2i) if and only if it divides Den(B_i). So in terms of their p-adic valuation the D_i are determined by the formula
    </p>
    <div class="equation">
      v_p(D_i) = \begin{cases} 1 + v_p(i+1) & \text{if } i+1 \equiv 0 \pmod{p-1} \\ 0 & \text{if } i+1 \not\equiv 0 \pmod{p-1} \end{cases}
    </div>
    <p>
      and D_1 = 2^3, D_3 = 2^3 \cdot 3.5, D_5 = 2^3 \cdot 3.2^7 ...
    </p>
  </div>
</div>

<div class="page">
  <div style="margin-top: 1em;">
    <p>
      A closely related result, also proved in [GMT], is the following theorem which was first conjectured by Akita [Ak] and which motivated Theorem 6.1.
    </p>
    <h3 style="font-size: 12pt; font-weight: bold;">Theorem 6.2.</h3>
    <p>
      The mod p reduction k_i in H^2(BT^∞; \mathbb{F}_p) vanishes if and only if i+1 ≡ 0 mod (p-1).
    </p>
    <p>
      We offer some remarks on these theorems and their proofs but need to refer to [GMT] for the details.
    </p>
    <p>
      The "only if" part of Theorem 6.2 is an immediate consequence of Theorem 6.1. For if k_i = 0 in H^2(BT^∞; \mathbb{F}_p) then p divides k_i, and in particular its reduction to the free part. So we must have v_p(D_i) ≠ 0. The "if" part of Theorem 6.2 follows by a computation in the Stiefel-Whitney classes and their odd prime analogue, the Wu classes.
    </p>
    <p>
      Vice versa, Theorem 6.2 immediately implies that D_2i ≥ 2 in Theorem 6.1. The lower bound in the odd case follows from (4.1). To prove that these are also the correct upper bounds certain surface bundles with structure groups Z/p^n for primes p and any integer n are considered.
    </p>
    <p>
      Clearing denominators in equation (4.1) one is naturally led to ask whether the relation between s_{2i-1} and k_{2i-1} holds in integral cohomology, see [Ak]. However, Madsen [Mad2] has recently shown that this is not the case. He proves nevertheless that the k_i classes can be replaced by some rationally equivalent classes k'_i such that the integral equation holds for these.
    </p>
  </div>
</div>

<div class="page">
  <div style="margin-top: 1em;">
    <h3 style="font-size: 12pt; font-weight: bold;">6.2. Comparison with H^*(BU).</h3>
    <p>
      Mumford's conjecture states in particular that the stable rational cohomology of the mapping class group is formally isomorphic to the rational cohomology of the infinite complex Grassmannian manifold, BU. We can make this relation more precise. Indeed, we can describe a map that induces this rational isomorphism as follows.
    </p>
  </div>
</div>

<div class="page">
  <div style="margin-top: 1em;">
    <h3 style="font-size: 12pt; font-weight: bold;">19</h3>
    <p style="text-align: right; font-size: 10pt;">ULRIKE TILLMANN</p>
  </div>

  <div style="margin-top: 1em;">
    <p>
      Let L : CP^∞ → BU be the map that classifies the canonical (complex) line bundle on CP^∞. By Bott periodicity, BU is an infinite loop space. Hence the map L can be extended to a map from the free infinite loop space on CP^∞ = BSO(2) using the universal property. On composition with α and ω one gets a map
    </p>
    <div class="equation">
      Z × BT^∞ → Ω^MSO(2) ⊗ Z → Ω^MSO(2) ⊗ Z × BU.
    </div>
    <p>
      Each of these maps induces an isomorphism in rational cohomology: α is a homotopy equivalence; ω is by Proposition 5.2 part of a fiber sequence where one of the terms Ω^MSO(2) ⊗ Z is split surjective with a fiber that has only trivial rational cohomology, and it is well-known that L is split surjective with a fiber that has only torsion cohomology, see [S2]. In [MT] we showed that through this map the kappa classes correspond to the integral Chern character classes.
    </p>
    <div class="equation">
      k_i = α*(ω*(L^i(cl_i))).
    </div>
    <p>
      This rational isomorphism between cohomology groups can be strengthened. Work-ing p-locally the following result is obtained in [GMT].
    </p>
    <div class="equation">
      Theorem 6.4. For odd primes p there is an isomorphism of Hopf algebras over the p-local integers
      </div>
    <div class="equation">
      H_free(BT^∞; \mathbb{Z}_{(p)}) ≅ H^*(BU; \mathbb{Z}_{(p)}).
    </div>
    <p>
      This fails for p = 2 where the algebra H_free(BT^∞; \mathbb{Z}_{(2)}) is not polynomial.
    </p>
  </div>
</div>

<div class="page">
  <div style="margin-top: 1em;">
    <h3 style="font-size: 12pt; font-weight: bold;">6.3. Torsion classes and F_p-homology.</h3>
    <p>
      When working with F_p-coefficients we are able to find infinitely many families of torsion classes in the stable homology. Each family is essentially a copy of H_*(BSO(2); F_p) = H_*(SO(2); F_p) from Example 3.2.1. We will describe these classes with reference to the fibration sequence in Proposition 5.2 and the map ω.
    </p>
    <p>
      Every infinite loop space has a product, and hence its homology has an induced product. Just as Steenrod operations measure for topological spaces the failure of the cup product in cohomology to be commutative at the chain level, so there are Dyer-Lashof operations that measure for infinite loop spaces the failure of the product in homology to be commutative at the chain level.
    </p>
    <p>
      To be more precise, consider sequences I = (e1, s1, ..., e_k, s_k) of non-negative integers with
    </p>
    <div class="equation">
      e_j ∈ {0, 1}, \quad s_j ≥ e_j, \quad p s_j - e_j ≥ s_{j-1},
    </div>
    <p>
      and define
    </p>
    <div class="equation">
      c(I) = 2s_1 - e_1 - \sum_{j=2}^k (2s_j(p-1) - e_j),
      \quad d(I) = \sum_{j=1}^k (2s_j(p-1) - e_j).
    </div>
    <p>
      For any infinite loop space Z and each I there is a homology (Dyer-Lashof) opera-tion,
    </p>
    <div class="equation">
      Q^I : H_*(Z; \mathbb{F}_p) → H_{d(I)}(Z; \mathbb{F}_p).
    </div>
  </div>
</div>

<div class="page">
  <div style="margin-top: 1em;">
    <h3 style="font-size: 12pt; font-weight: bold;">20</h3>
    <p style="text-align: right; font-size: 10pt;">ULRIKE TILLMANN</p>
  </div>

  <div style="margin-top: 1em;">
    <p>
      which can be non-zero only if e(I) + b(I) ≥ q.
    </p>
    <p>
      We can now describe H_*(Ω^MSO(2); \mathbb{F}_p) as the free Dyer-Lashof algebra on generators a_i ∈ H_*(BSO(2); \mathbb{F}_p). Explicitly, if
    </p>
    <div class="equation">
      T = {Q^I | a_i ≥ 0, e(I) + b(I) > 2q},
    </div>
    <p>
      where Λ(T) denotes the free graded commutative F_p-algebra generated by T, see [CLM; p. 42].
    </p>
    <p>
      By constructing certain surface bundles with finite structure groups Z/p^n a par-tial splitting (after p-adic completion) of the map
    </p>
    <div class="equation">
      ω: Z × BT^∞ → Ω^MSO(2) ⊗ Z.
    </div>
    <p>
      was constructed in [MT], and the following could be deduced.
    </p>
    <div class="equation">
      Proposition 6.6. The mod p homology H_*(BT^∞; \mathbb{F}_p) contains the free commuta-tive algebra Λ(T^*) where
    </div>
    <div class="equation">
      T^* = {Q^I | a_i ∈ T | q ≠ -1 (mod p - 1)}.
    </div>
    <p>
      A subset of the family associated to a_i had previously been found by Charney and Lee [CL]. Using the infinite loop space structure on BT^∞, the whole family could be detected, see [T2]. A complete description of the F_p-homology of Ω^MSO(2) has been given in [G1]. Galatius' computation is a careful analysis of the fibration in Proposition 5.2 and related spaces using amongst other tools the Ellenberg-Moore spectral sequence.
    </p>
    <p>
      <span data-field="theorem_6_7_statement">{{ theorem_6_7_statement }}</span>
    </p>
    <div class="equation">
      χ(Γ) = \frac{e(\Gamma)}{|\Gamma : \Gamma'|}.
    </div>
    <p>
      where e(Γ) = Σ_{g ∈ Γ} (-1)^{\text{dim} H_*(\Gamma; \mathbb{Q})} denotes the ordinary Euler characteristic of Γ. In the 1980s Harer and Zagier [HZ] computed the orbifold Euler characteristic of the mapping class group.
    </p>
    <div class="equation">
      Theorem 6.7. As before, let B_g denote the gth Bernoulli number. Then
    </div>
    <div class="equation">
      χ(Γ_g) = (-1)^{g+1} \frac{(2g + s - 3)!}{2g(2g - 2)!} B_g.
    </div>
  </div>
</div>

<div class="page">
  <div style="margin-top: 1em;">
    <h3 style="font-size: 12pt; font-weight: bold;">21</h3>
    <p style="text-align: right; font-size: 10pt;">ULRIKE TILLMANN</p>
  </div>

  <div style="margin-top: 1em;">
    <p>
      From this Harer and Zagier deduced a formula for the ordinary Euler character-istic for Γ_g and concluded that the Betti numbers grow exponentially. Furthermore, the Euler characteristic is often negative and hence there must be a large number of odd dimensional classes. By the confirmation of the Mumford conjecture, we now know that all these odd-dimensional classes must be unstable and that the stable classes form only a small proportion of the cohomology classes.
    </p>
  </div>

  <div style="margin-top: 1em;">
    <h3 style="font-size: 12pt; font-weight: bold;">7. Towards a proof.</h3>
    <p>
      We will restrict ourselves to defining the map α and outlining the proof as pre-sented in [GMTW] which is a simplification of the original one in [MW].
    </p>
    <h4 style="font-size: 11pt; margin-top: 1em;">7.1. The map α</h4>
    <p>
      To motivate the generalized Mumford conjecture and the definition of the map α it is helpful to recall the construction of the k_i classes from Section 4.1. For a smooth surface bundle E → B over a manifold B with fiber F, let T^E denote the vertical tangent bundle with Euler class e ∈ H^2(E; Z). Then,
    </p>
    <div class="equation">
      k_i = (-1)^{i+1} \pi_*(e^{i+1}) \in H^{2i}(B; Z).
    </div>
    <p>
      We used to characteristic classes as cohomology classes of some universal vector bundle V → B that is classified by a map f: B → BU its i-th Chern class c_i(V) = f^*(c_i) is the pull-back of the universal Chern class c_i ∈ H^{2i}(BU; Z). Similarly, one would like to interpret the k_i classes as pull-backs from some universal space.
    </p>
    <p>
      The definition of k_i above leads us to consider a wrong way map B → E followed by the map f: E → Gr(2,∞) that classifies the vertical tangent bundle. Such a wrong way map can be constructed via the Thom collapse map as follows. Embed the bundle E in R^N such that the projection onto B corresponds to π: E → B. Choose a suitable neighborhood of E in R^N × B such that it can be identified with the fiberwise normal bundle N^E of E in R^N × B, which is complementary to the vertical tangent bundle T^E. Taking Thom spaces (or one-point compactifications) and collapsing the outside of the neighborhood to a point gives a map
    </p>
    <div class="equation">
      Th(R^N × B) ≅ Σ^N(B_+) → Th(N^E).
    </div>
    <p>
      where B_+ as before denotes B with a disjoint basepoint. We compose this with the map
    </p>
    <div class="equation">
      f_E ≅ Th(N^E) → Th(U_{2,N-2}).
    </div>
    <p>
      induced by the map that classifies the vertical tangent bundle on E. Taking adjoints and letting N → ∞ we get the desired map
    </p>
    <div class="equation">
      α: B → Ω^MSO(2) = \lim_{N→∞} Ω^N(U_{2,N-2}).
    </div>
  </div>
</div>

<div class="page">
  <div style="margin-top: 1em;">
    <h3 style="font-size: 12pt; font-weight: bold;">22</h3>
    <p style="text-align: right; font-size: 10pt;">ULRIKE TILLMANN</p>
  </div>

  <div style="margin-top: 1em;">
    <div class="equation">
      (7.1)
    </div>
    <p>
      from section 5.1. A calculation just as in the proof of Proposition 5.3 (only easier) shows that for any connected space X of finite type
    </p>
    <div class="equation">
      πk(Ω^MSO(X)) ⊗ Q = Q[H_k(X) ⊗ Q].
    </div>
    <p>
      and
    </p>
    <div class="equation">
      H^*(Ω^MSO(X)) ⊗ Q = Λ(H^*(X) ⊗ Q).
    </div>
    <p>
      In particular
    </p>
    <div class="equation">
      H^*(Ω^MSO(BSO(2))) ⊗ Q = Q[s_0, s_1, s_2, ...],
    </div>
    <p>
      where each s_i has degree 2i and corresponds under this isomorphism to e^i ∈ H^{2i}(BSO(2)). We summarize our discussion with the identities
    </p>
    <div class="equation">
      k_i = π_*(e^{i+1}) = trf(e^i) = ω^i(s_i).
    </div>
    <p>
      The above discussion gives the last identity only rationally. But it holds also inte-grally; see [MT] or [GMT].
    </p>
  </div>
</div>

<div class="page">
  <div style="margin-top: 1em;">
    <h3 style="font-size: 12pt; font-weight: bold;">23</h3>
    <p style="text-align: right; font-size: 10pt;">ULRIKE TILLMANN</p>
  </div>

  <div style="margin-top: 1em;">
    <p>
      A subset of the family associated to a_i had previously been found by Charney and Lee [CL]. Using the infinite loop space structure on BT^∞, the whole family could be detected, see [T2]. A complete description of the F_p-homology of Ω^MSO(2) has been given in [G1]. Galatius' computation is a careful analysis of the fibration in Proposition 5.2 and related spaces using amongst other tools the Ellenberg-Moore spectral sequence.
    </p>
  </div>
</div>

<div class="page">
  <div style="margin-top: 1em;">
    <h3 style="font-size: 12pt; font-weight: bold;">24</h3>
    <p style="text-align: right; font-size: 10pt;">ULRIKE TILLMANN</p>
  </div>

  <div style="margin-top: 1em;">
    <p>
      7.3. Cobordism categories and their classifying spaces.
    </p>
    <p>
      The constructions and computations above make equally good sense for a closed oriented manifold W of any dimension d. In particular we have a map
    </p>
    <div class="equation">
      α: M^{top}(W) → Ω^MSO(d).
    </div>
    <p>
      In case of a manifold (with or without boundary) which is embedded in [a_0, a_1] × R^{d-1} we can modified the construction so that α(W) is now a map from [a_0, a_1] × R^{d-1} to Ω^MSO(d).
    </p>
    <div class="equation">
      M^{top}(W) → map([a_0, a_1], Ω^MSO(d)).
    </div>
    <p>
      These maps fit together to define a functor
    </p>
    <div class="equation">
      Cob_d → P(Ω^{d-1}MSO(d)).
    </div>
    <p>
      from the d-dimensional cobordism category (as defined in section 2.3) to the path category of Ω^{d-1}MSO(d). Recall the objects in the path category of a space X are the points in X. The morphism space P_X(x, y) is the space of continuous paths in X from x to y. Furthermore, the classifying space of P_X is homotopic to X. (This can be proved by an application of Theorem 3.3.) Let α denote again the map induced on classifying spaces:
    </p>
    <div class="equation">
      α: Cob_d → B(P(\Omega^{d-1}MSO(d))) ≅ Ω^{d-1}MSO(d).
    </div>
    <p>
      The main theorem of [GMTW] states that this map is a weak homotopy equivalence.
    </p>
    <h4 style="font-size: 11pt; margin-top: 1em;">Idea of proof:</h4>
    <p>
      There are essentially two steps. First, it is not too difficult to show that Cob_d is weakly homotopy equivalent to the space T_d of embedded d-dimensional manifolds without boundary that are closed subsets of the infinite tube R^d × [0, 1]. The topology on T_d is here such that manifolds can be pushed away to infinity (unlike in the topology used when defining our topological moduli spaces in section 2.1). One can construct a map as follows. T_d is the realization of a constant simplicial space and Cob_d is the realization of the nerve of the category of cobordisms. The latter defines an element in T after extending the composed cobordism to both sides by gluing infinite cylinders to its boundaries. This is indeed a weak homotopy equivalence on n-manifolds and hence on the realization.
    </p>
    <p>
      Secondly, by an adaptation of the classical arguments in cobordism theory (see [St]), one can now use transversality and Phillips's submersion theorem [P] to show that an element of π_n(Ω^MSO(d)) gives rise to a cobordism class of a triple (E, π, f) where π: E → S^n is a submersion and f: E → R is proper, and hence an element in π_n(T_d). This defines an isomorphism between the homotopy groups and hence the result follows.
    </p>
  </div>
</div>

    <div class="page">
        <header style="display: flex; justify-content: space-between; align-items: center; margin-bottom: 0.5em;">
            <h1 style="font-size: 14pt; margin: 0;">MUMFORD'S CONJECTURE - A TOPOLOGICAL OUTLOOK</h1>
            <span style="font-size: 12pt;">25</span>
        </header>
        <p style="margin-top: 1em;">
            <span class="section-header">Theorem 7.2.</span> <span data-field="theorem_statement">{{ theorem_statement }}</span>
        </p>
        <p>
            <span class="section-header">Idea of proof:</span> The proof is quite technical but essentially consists of a surgery argument the basic idea of which is quite easy to explain. Work with the space <span data-field="space_name">{{ space_name }}</span> as in the proof of Theorem 7.1. Essentially one wants to show that the space of all manifolds of dimension <span data-field="dimension">{{ dimension }}</span> has the same homotopy type as the space of manifolds without certain local maxima (relative to the projection onto the first coordinate) as they would give rise to cobordisms that are not in <span data-field="cobordism_group">{{ cobordism_group }}</span>. (The inverse of the weak equivalence <span data-field="equivalence_name">{{ equivalence_name }}</span> given in the proof of Theorem 7.1 takes a manifold <span data-field="manifold_type">{{ manifold_type }}</span> in <span data-field="range_set">{{ range_set }}</span> and restricts it to <span data-field="subset_set">{{ subset_set }}</span> for some transversal walls <span data-field="wall_count">{{ wall_count }}</span>, i.e., <span data-field="restriction_notation">{{ restriction_notation }}</span>. Grab these forbidden local maxima on <span data-field="space_name">{{ space_name }}</span> and pull to the right along the first coordinate axis so that in a neighborhood of each local maxima the manifold grows a very long nose reaching to +∞. Again it is important here that the topology on <span data-field="space_name">{{ space_name }}</span> is such that manifolds 'disappear' at infinity.
        </p>
        <p>
            This result provides the link between Theorem 7.1 and Theorem 4.6. Together they prove the generalized Mumford conjecture, Theorem 5.1:
        </p>
        <div class="equation">
            <span data-field="equation_content">{{ equation_content }}</span>
        </div>
    </div>
    <div class="page">
        <header style="display: flex; justify-content: space-between; align-items: center; margin-bottom: 0.5em;">
            <span style="font-size: 12pt;">26</span>
            <h1 style="font-size: 14pt; margin: 0;">ULRIKE TILLMANN</h1>
        </header>
        <p>
            We emphasize that the first homotopy equivalence depends crucially on homology stability which allowed us to apply the group completion theorem for categories. The homology stability theorem also allows us to state the following immediate consequence. Recall from section 3.2 that for every category <span data-field="category_name">{{ category_name }}</span> there is map <span data-field="map_name">{{ map_name }}</span> from the morphism spaces between two objects <span data-field="object_a">{{ object_a }}</span> and <span data-field="object_b">{{ object_b }}</span> to the space of paths from <span data-field="object_a">{{ object_a }}</span> to <span data-field="object_b">{{ object_b }}</span>. Taking <span data-field="value">{{ value }}</span> this gives a map from <span data-field="morphism_space">{{ morphism_space }}</span> to <span data-field="homotopy_space">{{ homotopy_space }}</span> which is of course the map $\alpha$ defined above. Furthermore, the computations in section 7.2 show that the component of the image is determined by (half) the Euler characteristic, <span data-field="characteristic">{{ characteristic }}</span>. We thus have the following result.
        </p>
        <p>
            <span class="section-header">Corollary 7.3.</span> The map <span data-field="map_name">{{ map_name }}</span> induces an isomorphism in homology for degrees <span data-field="degree_range">{{ degree_range }}</span>.
        </p>
        <p>
            <span class="section-header">7.3.1 Relation to Segal's category S:</span> An embedded, oriented surface <span data-field="surface_type">{{ surface_type }}</span> ∈ <span data-field="set_name">{{ set_name }}</span> inherits a metric and hence an induced almost complex structure. There is a unique complex structure that is compatible with this almost complex structure. By assigning to <span data-field="surface_type">{{ surface_type }}</span> this complex curve we can define a functor
        </p>
        <div class="equation">
            <span data-field="functor_notation">{{ functor_notation }}</span>
        </div>
        <p>
            For the subcategories <span data-field="subcategories">{{ subcategories }}</span> and <span data-field="subcategories">{{ subcategories }}</span>, <span data-field="functor_description">{{ functor_description }}</span> induces a homotopy equivalence between all morphisms spaces <span data-field="morphism_spaces">{{ morphism_spaces }}</span> and <span data-field="morphism_spaces">{{ morphism_spaces }}</span> because for surfaces with boundary the topological and Riemann's moduli spaces have the same homotopy type, see (2.2). Indeed, it is also not hard to see that <span data-field="functor_name">{{ functor_name }}</span> induces the homotopy equivalence of classifying spaces in Theorem 4.6. However, this is not the case for
        </p>
    </div>
